WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:04.376
The theory of limits is one of the branches of
mathematical

2
00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:07.540
analysis. The topic of computing limits is
very broad,

3
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:14.140
because there are many ways to find different
limits.

4
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:20.660
The limit of a function at a given point is
the value that the function approaches

5
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:27.580
as its argument approaches that point. It is
one of the

6
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.260
basic notions of mathematical analysis.

7
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:38.050
Consider the function y = f(x) in the
coordinate plane Oxy. Let

8
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:44.750
the function y = f(x) be defined in a
neighborhood of the point a, except

9
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:50.840
possibly at a itself. We are interested in how
this function behaves

10
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.890
as x approaches a.

11
00:00:53.890 --> 00:01:00.870
If for any given arbitrarily small positive
number ε

12
00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.350
there is a positive number δ depending on ε

13
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.750
such that for all x satisfying |x − a| &lt; δ

14
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:13.990
the inequality |f(x) − A|

15
00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:20.570
&lt; ε holds, then the number A is called the
limit of f(x)

16
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:30.510
as x approaches a, and it is written like
this:

17
00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.340
the limit of f(x) as x → a equals A.

18
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:38.657
In the coordinate plane Oxy, consider the
function y = f(x).

19
00:01:38.657 --> 00:01:45.500
The number A is called the limit of f(x) as x
approaches infinity

20
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:51.177
if for any positive number ε there is a number
N

21
00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:56.380
such that for all x &gt; N we have |f(x) − A| &lt;

22
00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:02.920
ε. Then we say that the limit

23
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.320
of f(x) as x approaches infinity equals A.

24
00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:13.100
This is how it is written. When computing
limits, we use the following

25
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.995
limit theorems: the limit of a constant equals
the constant;

26
00:02:17.995 --> 00:02:22.400
a constant factor can be taken outside the
limit sign.

27
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.686
If the limit of the denominator is not zero,

28
00:02:36.686 --> 00:02:41.080
the limit of a fraction equals the ratio of
the limits of the functions.

29
00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:49.310
If the function f(x) is defined at the point
a, then

30
00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:55.620
the limit of f(x) equals f(a). To compute

31
00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:02.480
the limit of an expression, substitute the
limiting value for the variable

32
00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.680
and apply the limit theorems.

33
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:11.230
Let us look at an example: the limit of the
function 3/(2x − 3)

34
00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:18.320
as x → 4. We substitute four and find the
value of the function.

35
00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.120
We get the answer: three fifths.

36
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:26.233
To find limits of functions, you need to know
the notions

37
00:03:26.233 --> 00:03:29.410
of infinitely small and infinitely large
quantities.

38
00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:36.020
If the limit of a function f(x) as x → a is
zero,

39
00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.800
the function is called infinitely small (an
infinitesimal).

40
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.640
Here are the properties of infinitesimal
functions.

41
00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:48.830
First, the sum, difference and product of
infinitesimals

42
00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.690
are infinitesimal functions.

43
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:58.440
Second, the product of infinitesimal
quantities is an infinitesimal function.

44
00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:04.760
Third, the product of an infinitesimal and a
bounded function

45
00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:08.240
is an infinitesimal.

46
00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:15.200
Let the functions α(x) and β(x) be
infinitesimal as x → a,

47
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:21.400
that is, the limits of α(x) and β(x) are zero.

48
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:28.320
If the limit of α(x)/β(x) equals one,

49
00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.400
the functions are called equivalent
infinitesimals.

50
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.920
If the limit of a given function is infinity,
the function y = f(x)

51
00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:47.860
is called infinitely large as x → a. If, as x
→ a,

52
00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:54.860
the limit of the ratio of two infinitely large
functions equals one, the infinitely

53
00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:59.800
large functions are called equivalent as x →
a.

54
00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:04.264
Very often, substituting the limiting value of

55
00:05:04.264 --> 00:05:07.350
the variable into an expression gives various

56
00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.138
indeterminate forms: zero over zero,

57
00:05:10.138 --> 00:05:14.010
infinity over infinity, one to the power
infinity,

58
00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:20.270
and others. In these cases, before
substituting the limit of the variable,

59
00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.676
we must transform the expression so that the

60
00:05:23.676 --> 00:05:26.850
indeterminacy disappears, then substitute

61
00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:30.932
the limit for the variable and apply the limit

62
00:05:30.932 --> 00:05:34.570
theorems. Computing limits in these cases

63
00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.890
is called resolving the indeterminacy.

64
00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.641
An indeterminate form of the type infinity
over infinity

65
00:05:42.641 --> 00:05:45.780
arises when finding the limit of a rational
function

66
00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:52.590
as x approaches infinity; here P(x)

67
00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:59.590
and Q(x) are polynomials of degrees n and m
respectively. Such

68
00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.159
limits follow a pattern: if the degree of the
numerator is

69
00:06:03.159 --> 00:06:06.420
less than the degree of the denominator, the
limit is

70
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:13.090
zero; if the degrees are equal, the
coefficients at these degrees

71
00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.911
are divided by each other; if the degree of
the numerator

72
00:06:15.911 --> 00:06:17.890
is greater than that of the denominator,

73
00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:20.690
the limit is infinity.

74
00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:28.360
Let us look at an example. We are given the
limit of the function (3x − 4)/(x − 2)

75
00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.980
as x approaches infinity.

76
00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:36.710
Here we have an indeterminate form of the type
infinity over infinity.

77
00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:42.600
We factor out x to the highest power and
cancel the fraction.

78
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:49.020
Using the limit theorems, we get the following
expression.

79
00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:55.580
A number divided by infinity gives zero, and
the final answer is three.

80
00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:03.550
Let the limit of a function f(x) again be a
rational fraction. If we get

81
00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:06.270
the indeterminate form zero over zero,

82
00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:09.850
then the numerator and denominator of the
fraction

83
00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:14.820
are factored. Let us look at an example. To
find this limit,

84
00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:20.930
we factor the numerator and denominator of the
fraction. We factor the numerator;

85
00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:27.460
for this we use the theorem on factoring a
quadratic trinomial.

86
00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:34.100
We cancel x − 2 in the numerator and
denominator and, substituting two,

87
00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:40.100
obtain the limit. As a result, the limit of
the function equals one half.

88
00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:46.250
One of the main techniques used to compute
limits involving irrational expressions,

89
00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:52.370
when the quotient limit theorem cannot be
applied, is to move the irrationality

90
00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:59.380
from the numerator to the denominator or vice
versa. Let us discuss an example.

91
00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:03.329
Let the limit of a fraction be given. We need
to remove the irrationality

92
00:08:03.329 --> 00:08:06.440
in the numerator: we multiply the numerator
and denominator

93
00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.059
by the sum of the square root of x − 2 and the
number

94
00:08:10.059 --> 00:08:13.200
two. After the transformations, cancelling x −

95
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:19.560
6, we get the limit of the fraction and,
substituting six for x,

96
00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:24.120
get the answer: one quarter.

97
00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:29.041
To compute limits of expressions with
trigonometric

98
00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:31.680
functions, we use the first remarkable

99
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:38.960
limit: the limit of sin x / x as x approaches
zero equals one.

100
00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.340
We can also use the corollaries of the first
remarkable limit.

101
00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.271
Let us look at an example. Let the limit of
tan x /

102
00:08:49.271 --> 00:08:52.580
x be given. To resolve the zero-over-zero
form,

103
00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:59.380
we use the formula of the first remarkable
limit. To do this,

104
00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.800
we write tan x as the ratio of sin x to cos

105
00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.060
x. We single out the formula of the first

106
00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:12.380
remarkable limit. Since the cosine of zero
equals one,

107
00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:14.820
we get that the result equals one.

108
00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:20.165
To resolve the one-to-the-power-infinity form,

109
00:09:20.165 --> 00:09:23.360
it is convenient to use the second remarkable

110
00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:30.320
limit. As x approaches infinity, the limit has
the following form,

111
00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:37.300
and α tends to zero. As x approaches infinity,
the limit has the following form,

112
00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:44.100
and α tends to zero. Both formulas give e;

113
00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.341
approximately, e ≈ 2.7, Euler's number.

114
00:09:47.341 --> 00:09:53.740
When solving examples, it is useful to keep in
mind the following equalities.

115
00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:58.384
Let us look at an example. Let the limit of
the

116
00:09:58.384 --> 00:10:01.740
function (1 + 1/x) to the power 7x be given.

117
00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:05.575
We single out the formula of the second
remarkable

118
00:10:05.575 --> 00:10:08.720
limit in brackets, and as a result we get

119
00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.780
e to the seventh power.

120
00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:17.440
When finding limits of functions, we can
compare infinitesimals.

121
00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:26.200
Let α(x) and β(x) be infinitesimals as x → a,
and let m be the limit of their ratio.

122
00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:30.892
Then, first, if m is a finite nonzero number,

123
00:10:30.892 --> 00:10:36.940
the functions are called infinitesimals of the
same order.

124
00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:42.770
Second, if m equals zero, the function α(x)

125
00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:48.560
is called an infinitesimal of higher order
than β(x).

126
00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:55.380
Third, if m is infinity, then β(x), compared
with α(x),

127
00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:59.960
is called an infinitesimal of higher order.

128
00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:06.860
If m equals one, the functions α(x) and β(x)
are called equivalent

129
00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:10.240
infinitesimals.

130
00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:15.532
If α tends to zero, it is useful to keep in
mind

131
00:11:15.532 --> 00:11:18.550
the following equivalent infinitesimals.

132
00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:23.050
We write it like this: equivalence is denoted
by the tilde sign.

133
00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:30.900
A very important conclusion follows from the
above: if two infinitesimal

134
00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:37.840
functions are equivalent, one of them can be
replaced by the other. This

135
00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:42.640
technique is widely used in practice, and now
we will look at it in an example.

136
00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:45.332
Let us find the limit of this function.

137
00:11:45.332 --> 00:11:49.060
We replace the infinitesimal function in the
numerator

138
00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:55.460
by an equivalent one, where α equals 4x. Then

139
00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:59.146
the original limit takes the following form.

140
00:11:59.146 --> 00:12:02.580
We cancel x and get the following result.

141
00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:09.220
Here the numerator is of higher order than the
denominator, so the limit is zero.

142
00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:14.660
So, in this lesson we studied one of the most
important

143
00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.024
notions of mathematical analysis,

144
00:12:17.024 --> 00:12:21.680
the limit of a function, and ways of finding
limits of functions.

145
00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:28.640
We also learned what infinitely large and
infinitely small quantities are and how

146
00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:34.400
they help to find limits of various functions
efficiently.
